From: OmacVM
Subject: [PATCH] cocoa: direct to display (experimental): frames copied into a CAMetalLayer

What: with OMACVM_GL_DIRECT=1 (OmacVM.app: "Show directly on the
display", experimental, off by default) the view's layer is a
CAMetalLayer. QEMU draws each frame into its own surfaces as before
(lossless-compressed 8-bit, half float, partial draws), and the commit
thread copies the frame's surface with a Metal blit into one of the
layer's drawables (lossless-compressed where the 8-bit surfaces are: Core
Animation's private wantsIOSurfaceCompression, checked with
respondsToSelector, macOS 27 as the lossless surfaces) instead of setting
it as a plain CALayer's contents. The vsync queue, pacing, colour tags,
EDR settings, the band below a guest a few rows shorter than the window
and the sublayers stay as they are; the vsync queue holds a frame's
drawable with its surface. Drawables come from a queue of their own
(nextDrawable can wait); QEMU's thread takes the spare one without
waiting and treats "none yet" (or one of an old size or kind) as all
surfaces busy (the frame waits a refresh, as before). The layer's size
and kind change on that queue, never during a nextDrawable. A surface
stays in use until the GPU has copied it. Needs the vsync present and a
Metal device (dlsym, and Metal classes by name: Metal is not linked into
QEMU); GPU safe mode keeps the plain layer. A frame Metal cannot read
turns it off for the run (plain layer, one warning).
qemu.log: "cocoa: direct to display: on", and from Core Animation's own
status of the layer "the display shows the window itself" or "composited
by WindowServer (status ...)", once a minute how many frames went direct.

Why: macOS 27 shows a full-screen window on the display itself (no
WindowServer composite: it neither reads the frame nor writes its own
framebuffer) only when its layer is a CAMetalLayer and no other window
covers any part of it; a CALayer showing IOSurfaces is always composited,
whatever the surface's kind, tags or Core Animation's private switches.
sRGB tags, scaled modes, our own sublayers, half-float and compressed
drawables and a window over the menu-bar strip (Omanotch's) keep it
direct; any window over the VM's window (a 1x1 pt one is enough)
composites again. The first version had GL draw straight into the
drawables: shown directly, but GL cannot draw into compressed drawables,
so it lost the lossless surfaces and partial draws and the whole Mac used
the same or more power (MacBook Air M2, the real VM at 60 Hz). Copying
keeps both; in a window (composited) it costs one extra copy a frame.

Upstream: OmacVM only (QEMU's IOSurface present is OmacVM's).
Test: app/runtime/Tests/display/test-present-direct.sh (the wiring of the
patched ui/cocoa.m; the checks catch code without it and the first
version). work/direct-display (ddbench, the Air rows) for the numbers.
Applies after omacvm-cocoa-pointer-bright.patch (before omacvm-cocoa-no-app-nap.patch).
